  "narrative": "<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Short impact summary</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Ma\u00efka De Keyzer\u2019s <i>Tot de bodem</i> is already showing signs of use beyond publication: it has 3,997 recorded downloads, has been discussed in a KU Leuven video event, and has one scholarly citation from a 2024 article in <i>Frontiers in Sustainable Food Systems</i>. The record suggests an early but real footprint in debates on agriculture, sustainability, and rural development.</p>\n<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Full narrative</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\"><i>Tot de bodem</i> (2023, Leuven University Press) enters a timely and contested policy landscape, engaging questions about the future of agriculture in Flanders and Europe, including nitrogen pressure, drought, biodiversity loss, seasonal labor, and meat production and consumption. Its impact record points to a book that is being read and taken up in practice-oriented scholarly conversations, even if the citation base is still small.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The strongest signal in the record is usage: the book has been downloaded 3,997 times through OAPEN, with especially heavy activity early on and continued access afterward. That pattern indicates active readership rather than only a one-time spike, and it is consistent with a work that is being consulted as part of ongoing discussion about land use and agricultural transition.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">There is also an early sign of scholarly uptake. OpenAlex records one citation, in a 2024 research article on the agroecological performance and sustainability of community supported agriculture farms in Flanders. That citing article sits squarely in the book\u2019s topical area, connecting the book to current work on organic food and agriculture, land use, and rural development.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Beyond formal citation, the book has been brought into public academic discussion through a KU Leuven video debate on the future of agriculture in Flanders, indicating that it has been used as a reference point in broader institutional conversation. Repository and library records also show open availability and catalog presence, which support discoverability and access, while the funding record notes KU Leuven support.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Overall, the evidence portrays a book with clear early reach: substantial downloads, one relevant citation, and visible use in a university debate. The impact profile is still emerging, but the available record already shows that <i>Tot de bodem</i> is circulating in both scholarly and public-facing discussions on sustainable agriculture.</p>",
